# Line endings for artifacts whose BYTES are the contract.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is the Git-for-Windows installer default, it is set on this project's
# development machine, and it is the default on GitHub's `windows-latest` runners — which is the
# runner the licensing gates run on. With no attributes declared, git converts LF to CRLF on
# checkout for every file it detects as text. Measured on a simulated fresh checkout of this
# repository:
#
#   NOTICE                                  657 CRLF pairs  -> `npm run check:notice` fails
#   sbom.cdx.json                        17,452 CRLF pairs  -> `npm run check:sbom` fails
#   licensing/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt   22,478 CRLF pairs  -> `npm run check:licenses` fails
#   schemas/cyclonedx/bom-1.5.schema.json          sha256 067f7824... -> e03d2164...
#
# Every one of those is a false failure telling the developer to regenerate a file that is already
# correct, and regenerating would produce LF again and leave the tree permanently dirty. The last is
# worse than a false failure: the SHA-256 in `schemas/cyclonedx/PROVENANCE.json` exists to detect a
# schema someone edited, so a checkout would fire it as a **tampering alarm** on an untouched file.
#
# The generators emit LF unconditionally, on every platform, and the `--check` gates compare bytes.
# So these paths are pinned rather than left to autodetection.
#
# Scoped deliberately. A repository-wide `* text=auto eol=lf` would renormalise every tracked file
# and is a separate decision with a much larger diff; this fixes the paths where the byte sequence
# is load-bearing, and `tests/frontend/thirdPartyLicenses.test.ts` asserts the pinning is still here.

# Generated compliance artifacts — byte-compared by `--check` gates.
NOTICE                      text eol=lf
sbom.cdx.json               text eol=lf
licensing/**                text eol=lf

# `THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt` reproduces third-party licence texts verbatim, and some of them contain
# trailing spaces — `ring`'s BoringSSL notice, `image`'s exrs attribution, several npm MIT texts that
# were hard-wrapped by hand. `git diff --check` reports each one as a whitespace error, which it is
# not: the bytes are quoted material, `licensing/third-party-index.json` records the SHA-256 of the
# source file, and "tidying" them would falsify a text whose exact reproduction is the obligation.
licensing/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt  text eol=lf whitespace=-trailing-space

# Vendored upstream schemas — hashed against PROVENANCE.json. `-text` rather than `text eol=lf`:
# these must round-trip exactly as vendored, so git is told not to transform them in either
# direction rather than told which transform to apply.
schemas/**                  -text
